// The boss journal: Grubnak, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).grubnak = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Grubnak runs a kitchen like a bad parade ground: grease on the flagstones, fire in the corners, and a sergeant with two cleavers who never stands down. I lost my footing twice before I understood the room. Laced my boots tighter tonight. We go back at dawn.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook. Fire along the floor, oil underfoot, cleavers for whoever stands in front. I have been struck by worse and kept my post. What I mind is the sliding; a dead man should at least be allowed to stand still. We go back in tomorrow.",
    thresher: "Grubnak keeps his kitchen as a host keeps a long dinner: every course arriving at once, nobody permitted to leave. Greasy floor, a hot stove, a cleaver for whoever stands in front. We were not ready for him yet. Time enough to learn the steps.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one hits like a side of beef falling off its hook, and never stops serving. Grease, fire, cleavers, all of it in my face. I am in love. Tomorrow I bleed for him properly, and he can keep the apron.",
    runeguard: "The Chef is legible once one knows his instruments; each cleaver, ladle and lid is written on him before it falls. Tonight I copied out only the first page, and misread half. Tomorrow I will have the whole text, and the margin notes besides.",
    gorgemaw: "At last, a kitchen that never stops sending out plates. My host's menu is long and his hand with the seasoning heavy, and the grease is a crime against honest fat. I sampled everything and lost my chair. Tomorrow I eat properly.",
    oathbroker: "I have read the Ogre Chef's terms and found them long: a continuous service, every course payable in full, no clause for rest. My co-signatories defaulted early, and so did I. Tomorrow the fine print is read before the first plate, not after.",
    ironpalm: "The cook speaks fast and never waits for an answer. Cleaver, ladle, lid, fire, and no pause between. We heard half of it tonight and answered less. I will find his silences tomorrow. Everyone has silences.",
    ranger: "Ogre in a long room. Greasy floor, casks along the walls for cover, fire in the corners. He never stops cooking, so there is no resting shot. Lost my footing, then my lane. Need to pace the place out before we go in again.",
    shadowblade: "My mark this time is an ogre in an apron, waving cleavers at whoever stands in front of him. Charming. He turns far too often for my taste, but he has a back like a barn door, and I found it. Twice.",
    pyromancer: "Grubnak cooks with fire, and badly! The whole kitchen smoked and spat, and I spent more time running from it than casting. An amateur with a stove. My fringe will never forgive him, and neither will I.",
    thermist: "Kitchen: hot. Ogre: hotter. Grease underfoot. I read it cold, clipped, sensible, and then everything came at once, flames and plates and cleavers, and I boiled over before I had written the first line. Too far. Steadier tomorrow.",
    driftblade: "The cook never stops and neither did I, through the grease and round the casks and out past his cleavers, and it was glorious right up to the moment the floor caught fire under me. I have never been so happy to be hurt. Again tomorrow.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man is an ogre in an apron, and the stage is a kitchen: grease on the boards, fire in the wings, and the company missing half its cues. My Doll took it badly. (Exit, pursued by a cleaver.) The blocking will be redone by morning.",
    cleric: "The ogre feeds his kitchen with heroes, and he very nearly fed it us. Every course reached all of us, and the grease underfoot made the faithful careless. I said the long prayer over the cold stove. We will be wiser tomorrow, and in better shoes.",
    grovekeeper: "Hm. A kitchen is its own weather, dear: grease falls like rain, fire rolls in like a dry summer, and not a breath of still air. I'll learn its seasons. My lot came home singed but sprouting. That's a fair start.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak serves in courses, each a little faster than the last, in the same order every time. I knew that already, of course, which is the trouble with knowing. Tonight we learned only the first bars of the tune.",
    alembist: "Ailment: one ogre with a kitchen, and a kitchen with opinions. Note for the shelf: he labels everything in advance and we did not. Collected a jar of the grease for study. It sparked. Two small fires, neither of them mine, I swear.",
    threadweaver: "That ogre ran us ragged round his greasy kitchen, and my little stitches wandered off to every corner of it. Ripped seams, frayed hems, and not one of them tied to anyone. Disgraceful needlework, mine. Tomorrow we hold the hem.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "Grubnak is down. Every course, every cleaver, and the line stood through all of it. I walked out of that kitchen on ruined boots and my own two feet. Post kept. The captain would have been proud.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook is down, and all of us still stand. I stayed a while by his cold stove. He fed many to that fire. I was fed to a different one, long ago, and it was kinder.",
    thresher: "Grubnak came in at last, the whole long service of him, and went down heavily onto his own kitchen floor. Every course came due, and we were standing where we should be for each. I will miss him a little. His name was on the list, and there was room.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one is down, and I have a scar from every course he served. Best meal I ever bled for. Shame it's over. Somebody tell the next one he has a standard to meet.",
    runeguard: "The Chef has fallen. Every sign came in its order, and every answer was ready a breath before it was needed. I have copied the whole text into my margins at last. As foreseen; though I will admit the third chapter surprised me.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is finished, and so is his menu: every course eaten, every dish sent back to him in pieces. I took his ladle as a keepsake. A long, heavy, mediocre meal, and I am sorry it has ended.",
    oathbroker: "The Ogre Chef's account is closed: every course paid in full by the undersigned, and his own balance collected at the last. I have filed the receipt. A second ledger lies open for the next creditor. Countersigned.",
    ironpalm: "The cook said everything he had to say. We answered every word. At the end he was quiet, and for the first time I could hear the kitchen breathing. I had no wish to speak into that silence. I bowed.",
    ranger: "Ogre's down. Whole service, every lane I could find, and not one wasted shot after the third course. Good hunt. Took a cask-stave as a trophy.",
    shadowblade: "The mark served his last course and toppled face first into it, glaring at our knight to the end. I lifted a silver spoon from his apron as I passed. A souvenir. It never saw me, even then.",
    pyromancer: "We beat Grubnak! I burned brighter than his whole stove, and for once the fire did what I told it. Left eyebrow: present. Right: negotiable. Tell the next cook I have opinions about his kitchen.",
    thermist: "Down. Kitchen cooling. I ran hot all night and somehow landed right in the middle every time. Readings steady, for once. Back to the middle, and stayed there. Marked the date on my glass.",
    driftblade: "We went through every course he had and out the other side, and he dropped mid-swing, and I didn't land once all evening. The kitchen is quiet now. I ran one lap of it just to hear my own feet.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man has taken his final bow, face down in the pudding. The company was magnificent; my Doll, unforgettable. A triumphant closing night. (Curtain. The Doll collects the cleaver as a prop.)",
    cleric: "The cook is gone, and every one of us walked out of his kitchen. I lit a candle by the cold stove for those who didn't, before us. He was hungry, in the end, like all of us. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The old ogre's season is over, dear. My lot weathered every course, and the kitchen is quiet now, like the morning after a storm. I left a pinch of sage by the cold stove. Something should grow there.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak fell on the last bar, as he was always going to. I'd say I knew, but this time I truly didn't have to undo a single thing, and that is a different pleasure. I hummed his tune on the way out.",
    alembist: "Ailment: one ogre chef. Remedy: patience, labels, and a party that stood where I told it. Shelves nearly empty, jar of grease rescued. One explosion, which was celebratory and therefore does not count.",
    threadweaver: "The ogre's down and not one thread snapped the whole service. My little stitches held from first course to last, and not a seam in the hem has gone. I would make him an apron, but he is too big. Pulled it tight.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Grease Fire': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Arms up, and the oil under the whole kitchen lit at once. Nothing for a shield to meet. We closed up on the healer, were mended before the flames rose, and stood in it together. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He raised his arms and the oil lit under every one of us. There was nothing in that room to stand behind, so we crowded the healer and were mended before the fire rose. I have been warmer than this, once, at the end.",
      thresher: "Up went his arms, and the oil took light beneath the whole kitchen at once. There is no stepping round a fire like that. We drew in about the healer and were mended ahead of it. It burns out; it always does.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole floor flared at once, and I barely felt it, which is shameful. The soft ones did, so we huddled at the healer's elbow and were mended before the grease caught. Warm work, though.",
      runeguard: "Arms raised, and the sheen of oil widens under every one of us: a sentence with no way out of it. The only answer is to stand by the healer and be whole before it catches. I wrote that in the margin before it burned.",
      gorgemaw: "Course the first: the whole floor set alight at once, no seat spared. There was no plate to send back, so we sat close to the healer and swallowed it together. Rancid oil, overcooked, and the cook seemed proud of it.",
      oathbroker: "It transpired, on first reading, that Grease Fire binds every co-signatory at once, and that no clause permits a party to decline. We gathered at the healer, made good our wounds in advance, and paid it in common. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "His arms rose. He spoke to all of us at once, and there was nowhere to stand outside the word. We gathered close, whole before he finished, and let the fire pass through. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Oil across the whole floor, then fire. No lane out of it, no cover worth the name. Closed in on the healer before it lit and took it standing. Hate being herded. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "When he lifted his arms the oil ran under everyone, which was frankly unkind. There is no hiding from a floor, so I slipped in beside the healer, had my scratches mended beforehand, and smiled through the smoke.",
      pyromancer: "The oil sheen rolled out under all of us, and I learned there is no outrunning a floor. So I planted myself beside the healer and kept casting while the flames came to me. Only my hem smouldered! Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Arms up, oil everywhere, no edge to step past. Cold sense says gather. Then the heat hit all of us and I flared, rushing, scalding, but we were together and whole, and it washed through. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Oil down under all of us and no way through, and I hate a floor with no way through, so I drifted in beside the healer and stood still, actually still, while it burned over and past us.",
      marionettist: "Cue: the ogre raises his arms and the whole stage goes up. No wings to hide in. The company gathered centre, mended beforehand, and took it as one. (The Doll, merely toasted.)",
      cleric: "He raised his arms and the grease beneath everyone caught alight. I drew them all in close and laid the light on them before the flames rose, not after. Mercy is cheaper early. Nobody fell. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Grease fire comes on like a dry summer, dear: the whole floor at once, no shade anywhere. I gathered my lot close and set my mending a breath ahead of it. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Arms up, oil out, fire a beat later; everyone burns, every time. We gathered on the upbeat and I had them mended before it caught. Next time, which was this time, nobody needed undoing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: oil under the whole party, lit a moment after his arms go up. Remedy: everyone gathered near me, wounds closed beforehand. Note: no dose cures it after; brew it first, and keep the brandy well away.",
      threadweaver: "Grease under every stitch at once. I drew the whole hem in close around me before it caught and shared the burn between us, evenly, the way a good seam shares strain. Nobody frayed.",
    } },
    'Mince': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver caught the light and that whole half of the kitchen was condemned. I kept his face on me while the line crossed to the dull half. Nobody on the bright side when it fell. A good drill.",
      gloomsworn: "One cleaver caught the light, and that half of the kitchen was spoken for. I kept his eyes on me while the others stepped over to the dull half. Nobody was buried today.",
      thresher: "One cleaver caught the light, and that half of the floor was the half due the blow. I strolled over to the dull half before it fell and went on working from there. Read the glint, cross the floor. Quite simple.",
      bloodreaver: "One cleaver glinted and that whole half of the room was about to get chopped. Tempting, very. But I crossed to the dull side like a good lad. Scars keep, and the big one will offer another.",
      runeguard: "A glint along one cleaver is the heading of the page: that half of the floor is the chapter he means to strike. Cross to the other before it falls. I have underlined the glint twice.",
      gorgemaw: "One flash of steel and he carved the room in halves, the lit half for the chop. Crude knife-work, no finesse. I moved my chair to the dull half and watched from a safe seat. Hacked, not cut.",
      oathbroker: "The glinting cleaver names the half of the room that pays; by the same clause the other half owes nothing at all. The undersigned moved accordingly, and the Chef collected from empty floor.",
      ironpalm: "A cleaver glinted. It said: this side. I answered by standing on the other. It fell where I was not, and sounded foolish.",
      ranger: "Watched which cleaver caught the light. That half got chopped. Crossed behind the line of it and waited, bow drawn. Not hard, once you know to look.",
      shadowblade: "One cleaver glittered at the mark's side, practically announcing itself. That half of the floor is his; the other half is mine. I strolled across and he chopped empty air. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "That cleaver glinted the colour of trouble, and I said so, loudly, a moment before the whole bright half of the kitchen was minced. I was already on the dull side, casting. Not a sleeve lost.",
      thermist: "Which cleaver shines, which half dies. Cross. Then he chopped and I was already flaring back in from the safe side, rushing, burning. The shiny half is too far, always.",
      driftblade: "One cleaver flashed and I was already round him and through to the other half, and the chop came down on empty stone behind me, and I was back at his side before the dust had finished rising.",
      marionettist: "One cleaver flashes in the footlights, and that half of the stage is struck. The company exits to the other half on cue. A clean change of scene, and my Doll on the dull side, unscathed.",
      cleric: "One of his cleavers flashed, and everything on that side was chopped. Our archer lingered on the wrong half once, and I scolded gently afterwards. Cross to the dull half the moment the glint shows, dears.",
      grovekeeper: "Mince splits the kitchen like a plough splits a field, dear. The glinting cleaver marks the half that gets turned over. My lot crossed to the other furrow early and stayed dry.",
      chronomancer: "The cleaver glints, a beat, then that whole half is gone. I had crossed to the dull side before the glint finished; I always will have. It is the only promptness I am proud of.",
      alembist: "Label: the glinting cleaver. Meaning: that half of the kitchen is about to be chopped. Dose: stand in the other half. I wrote it on the shelf in large letters, and underlined it in something that smoked.",
      threadweaver: "A cleaver winked on one side of that ogre, and he cut that half clean out of the pattern. I gathered my stitches on the other half before it fell. He cuts a poor line, but a straight one.",
    } },
    'Double Chop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights he chops both halves, the glinting side first, the other right behind it. We stood on the dull half, then crossed into the ground he had just cut. Fresh-cut floor does not get cut twice. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He cuts both halves, the bright one first and the dull one after, when the nights are harder. We waited on the dull side, then crossed into what he had just cut. The only safe place in that kitchen is behind the blade.",
      thresher: "He cuts the floor twice on the hard nights, glinting half first, the dull one after. I waited on the dull side, and when the first came down I stepped onto the half it had just struck. Ground already struck does not come due again.",
      bloodreaver: "Both halves, one after the other! I waited on the dull side, and the instant the glinting half came down I walked straight into it. Clever, my friend. Not clever enough.",
      runeguard: "Not foreseen; now it is. The glinting half falls first and the dull half is struck behind it; wait on the dull side, then cross to the freshly cut ground. I added a second column to the page. The order is the answer.",
      gorgemaw: "Unsure of his portions, the cook chopped both halves of the table in turn. I sat the first out on the dull side and moved to the cleared plate for the second. Fussy, and wasteful with the knife.",
      oathbroker: "On the hard nights a second instalment follows the first: the glinting half is due, and then the other. We waited on the later account, then crossed to the settled one, for a creditor cannot collect twice from the same floor.",
      ironpalm: "He said this side, then that side. I stood where he would speak second, then moved to where he had already spoken. A word is never said twice in the same place.",
      ranger: "Two chops. Glinting half first, then the other. Waited on the dull side, then cut across into the first. Ground he's finished with stays quiet.",
      shadowblade: "He chopped one half, then the other, like a man who cannot decide. I waited on the dull side and slipped across into the cut half the moment it fell. Indecision is so unbecoming.",
      pyromancer: "Both halves, one after the other! I stood on the dull side, then dashed into the glinting half the instant it was done, mid-cast. Two chops, no eyebrows lost. Well. One.",
      thermist: "Glint side, then the other side behind it. Wait on the other. Then hot: first chop lands, I cross over, burning, as the second slams down behind me. A hair's breadth. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "He chops one half and then the other, so I waited on the dull side, hating it, then went through into the cut ground the moment it fell, and the second chop landed in my wake.",
      marionettist: "A two-scene number: the glinting half is chopped, then the other. The company waits in the second, then crosses into the first on the downbeat. (The Doll, crossing on cue, to applause.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the cleaver falls twice: the glinting half, then the other. Wait on the dull side, then step into the ground that was cut first. I watched every friend manage it, and was quietly proud.",
      grovekeeper: "Double Chop is a field turned twice, dear. The glinting furrow first, the other after. My lot waited in the second and stepped into the first once it was turned, like rotating crops.",
      chronomancer: "Glinting half, then the echo on the other. I waited out the first beat on the dull side and was in the cut half for the second. A canon in two voices. Obvious in hindsight, which is the only sight I have.",
      alembist: "Two doses, one after another: the glinting half, then the other. Wait in the half that doesn't glint, then cross into the one that just fell. Order matters, as with reagents, and I have the eyebrows to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "Glinting half first, the other right behind it. I kept my stitches on the dull side, then drew them across into the cut half all together, no strays. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Ladle Spin': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He whirled the ladle round himself, scalding anything close. I stepped off him with the line, out past the edge, and came straight back in once it stopped. Gave ground on purpose. First time I have called a retreat a plan.",
      gloomsworn: "He swung the ladle about himself and scalded all who stood close. I walked away, the others with me, and returned the moment it stopped. Out, then in. I have done both in my time.",
      thresher: "Grubnak whirled his ladle and scalded everything near him. I stepped back past the edge of it, leaned on my scythe a moment, and walked back in when it stopped. No hurry.",
      bloodreaver: "Soup flew in a ring round his ladle, and I backed off for once. Hated it! Then straight back in for the next good hit, scalded boots and all.",
      runeguard: "The ladle swings low and soup churns about his feet: a ring is drawn before the spin. Step outside it; return once it has turned. The ring is the inscription, and the spin is only its reading aloud.",
      gorgemaw: "Soup, served by flinging it round in a circle. I declined, stepped back past the splash, and returned for the next course. Thin, and much too hot for the pleasure of it.",
      oathbroker: "The ladle binds only those who stand close, and the obligation lapses the moment the spin ends. The undersigned stepped beyond its terms, waited out the lapse, and returned none the poorer.",
      ironpalm: "The ladle said: step away. I stepped away. When it had finished speaking, I came back. Some words are not for answering.",
      ranger: "Ladle out, ring of soup round its feet. Backed off to the edge, waited out the spin, walked back in. A boar chasing its own tail.",
      shadowblade: "A tidy circle of soup, as if his back needed defending. I stepped out of reach, waited politely, and returned to it. Such good manners, on both sides.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of scalding soup round his feet, so I stepped well outside it and kept casting from there. I am always outside anyway. Splash-free, which is a first for my sleeves.",
      thermist: "Ladle low, soup churning. Step out. Wait. Then I swung right back in at his side, hot again, before the steam had cleared. Too eager. Fine.",
      driftblade: "He spun the ladle, and I slid out past the edge of the soup, a gust round a candle, and back through the steam the instant it stopped.",
      marionettist: "The ladle number: a ring of hot soup at centre stage, and the company exits upstage until the spin ends. Return for the bow, not a moment before. (The Doll, briefly in the wings.)",
      cleric: "When he swings the ladle, everything near him is scalded. Our knight and the other close fighters stepped back out of the ring, and I did not have to mend a single burn. A quiet little mercy.",
      grovekeeper: "The ladle spin is a little whirlwind at his feet. Easy weather to read: step out past the churned ground, wait, step back. My lot learned it quickly, and no one steamed.",
      chronomancer: "The ladle comes round once: out on the wind-up, back in on the beat after. A short, dull little tune. I have heard it more times than the cook has stirred it.",
      alembist: "Ailment: scalding soup in a ring round him. Remedy: stand outside the ring until the ladle stops. Note: soup, unlike brandy, does not explode. A pity.",
      threadweaver: "The ladle came round and my stitches stepped out of the soup all at once, nice and even, then came back in together. It is a pleasure when people move like one seam.",
    } },
    'Lid Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lifted the lid overhead and everything away from him took the slam. I stood under his chin and the line closed in behind me. Under him is the only safe ground in the room. Odd, but correct.",
      gloomsworn: "The lid went up, and everything away from him took the slam. The only shelter was under his chin, so I stood there and the others pressed in behind. The dark taught me to stand where the fear is.",
      thresher: "When the lid went up, the whole kitchen away from him came due at once, all but the ground beneath his chin. So I tucked in against him, close as a shadow, and let it fall on empty floor.",
      bloodreaver: "The lid went up and the shock rolled out over the whole room, except right at his belly. So in we came, nose to apron with my friend. Cosy.",
      runeguard: "The lid held aloft throws a shadow outward, and the quiet lies beneath it. Come in under him before it falls; the far ground is where the sentence lands. I trust the inscription over my own instincts here.",
      gorgemaw: "He lifted the lid on his dish and smacked the whole room with it, except those seated right at his elbow. I always sit close to the kitchen; one learns where the food is. Underwhelming.",
      oathbroker: "By an exemption nobody reads, those within arm's reach of the lid owe nothing to the slam, while everyone at a distance pays in full. I closed in with a clear conscience and a firm grip on my pen.",
      ironpalm: "The lid rose. It said: come close. We came close. It struck everything else, and we stood in the quiet it made.",
      ranger: "Lid up. Everywhere hit but right under it. Gave up my range and walked in. Ugly. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "The lid rose over the mark's head and the whole room beyond him was doomed. So I came in close, right behind him, where I'd rather be anyway. Safest seat in the house.",
      pyromancer: "Everywhere away from him gets flattened, so I had to run in beside the ogre, who smells awful, and then out again to burn. Hair: gravy-scented. I have decided to be brave about it.",
      thermist: "Lid overhead. Clipped: the quiet middle is under him. Close in. Then the slam rippled out past us and I was hot and cutting before it reached the walls. Closer than I like.",
      driftblade: "The lid rose and the safe place was in close, so I went straight in under it, nearly through his apron strings, and the slam rolled out through the room behind me.",
      marionettist: "Blocking for the lid: downstage, into his very shadow, where the slam takes the empty house. Everyone else is a corpse in the cheap seats. The Doll sat under his elbow, quite safe, and slightly smug.",
      cleric: "When he raised the lid, the safe place was right up against him, which frightened some of us. I walked in first so they would follow, and the slam passed over our heads. Courage is mostly company.",
      grovekeeper: "The lid slam is like hail: it falls everywhere but under the old tree. My lot crowded in at his feet before it fell. Funny shelter, that ogre, but a shelter is a shelter.",
      chronomancer: "Lid up, everyone in, and then the slam rolls out past us. The middle is quiet every time. I was standing there before I decided to.",
      alembist: "Ailment: everything away from him slammed flat. Remedy: stand close underneath. Note: counterintuitive. Label twice, in different inks.",
      threadweaver: "The lid went up and I drew every thread in tight to his boots before it fell. Anyone left slack at the edges would have been flattened. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Tenderize': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A red ring settled on me and followed me about while he wound up, then locked, and his ghost cleaver came down on it edge first. I braced in the last instant and took it whole; the others kept clear of my ring. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The ring ran after me until it locked, and the cleaver fell out of the air onto it, edge first. I drew the dark in and took it whole; there was nowhere to go and no reason to. The rest kept out of the ring.",
      thresher: "A red ring crept after our shield and set, and then the ghost of the cleaver came down on it, edge first, neat as a signature. The knight cannot step aside from that one, so he takes it and the rest of us stay well out of his circle. In time.",
      bloodreaver: "Tenderize! The ring stuck to me like a hound, locked, and down came his ghost cleaver, edge first, right through me. A good one; I took it full and drank it back, and the others kept out of my circle. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a red ring that follows me until it locks, and then the spectral cleaver falls upon it, edge first. It cannot be outrun, so the answer is a ward laid a breath before, while the rest keep outside the ring. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The signature dish, dropped from above onto my plate: his ghost cleaver, edge first, on the ring that trailed me. I could not step out of it, so I opened wide and swallowed it whole while my friends kept off my side of the table. Meaty.",
      oathbroker: "The circle trailed me until it locked, and the spectral cleaver fell upon it, edge first; the principal cannot evade, and any bystander within the circle is liable for a large share. I accepted delivery in full, and my co-signatories stayed outside the ring. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The ring followed me, then stopped. The cleaver came down out of the air, edge first; I could not step aside, so I did not try. I raised my hands at the last instant and took it, and the others stood outside my circle. Then I answered.",
      ranger: "Red ring on the knight, trailing him till it set. Ghost cleaver dropped on it, edge first, and he took it where he stood. Stayed well clear of his circle. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A red ring followed our shield about, then settled, and a ghost cleaver fell out of nowhere onto it, which is rather obvious of him. I stay well out of other people's circles. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A red ring chased our knight around like a puppy, and then his ghost cleaver dropped on it, edge first, huge as a door! The knight takes it, our healers lit him up beforehand, and I kept out of that circle, naturally. My eyebrows lived to see it.",
      thermist: "Cold read: ring on the shield, trailing, then locked. Cleaver drops, edge first, nothing for him to dodge; everyone else, out of his circle. I ran hot with worry, but the healers had him topped, and it passed. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The ring slid after our shield and then locked, and the ghost cleaver dropped on it, and I went round the edge of it, through the gaps and never in his circle, while the knight took it full because there was no running from that one.",
      marionettist: "His great moment: a red spotlight trailing the leading player until it sets, then the ghost cleaver drops from the flies, edge first. The knight takes the whole blow centre stage; the rest of the company keeps out of his light. (The Doll, upstage, admiring.)",
      cleric: "The red ring followed our knight about, and when it set, the ghost cleaver fell upon him, edge first. He cannot step aside, so I lay the light on him before it lands, not after, and the rest kept out of his circle. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "It came like hail on one tree: a red ring that trails our shield and then sets, and the ghost cleaver dropping on it. I planted my mending on him before it fell, and my lot stayed out of his circle. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The ring follows the knight, locks, and the cleaver arrives a beat later, edge first, onto where he already is. There's nothing to dodge, so I had mended him before it landed, and the rest stood out of his circle. Sensible, not clever.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one ghost cleaver, edge first, onto the ring that tracks our shield. Remedy: dose him before it lands, not after, and everyone else out of his circle. Note: do not stand beside the patient.",
      threadweaver: "The ring trailed our knight till it set, and the cleaver fell on it, edge first. I had tied his thread to mine already, so a little of the weight ran down the seam to me, and everyone else kept out of his circle. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Meat Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Grubnak hung a slab over each of the others, every one wanting its own patch of floor. They dropped theirs at the edges, apart, away from the line. Footing kept. The stain thins in time, shrinking and dimming, and is gone altogether if fire reaches it.",
      gloomsworn: "Slabs hung over the others, each wanting a place of its own, each leaving grease where it landed. They went to the edges and apart. The stain thins with time, and fire eats it at once. I remember where each fell.",
      thresher: "A slab hung over my head, darkening. I carried it off to the wall, away from the others, and let it drop where nobody stands. The patch fades in its own hour, shrinking and dimming, and fire takes it at once, so I mind where it lies.",
      bloodreaver: "He hung steaks over the others, never me, insulting. They each took a corner and left the grease out by the walls, not under my feet. Clean floor to bleed on. The stains shrink and dim after a while, or burn off if fire finds them.",
      runeguard: "A steak on a chain over each of us save the one before him; the grease it leaves is a mark that will outlast the page. Walk apart to the edge and let it fall there. It dims and shrinks with time, and fire ends it at once.",
      gorgemaw: "Steaks dangled over my friends but not me, which I took personally. They carried their plates apart, to the walls, so the grease would not pool under the table. The stains shrink and dim in the end, and fire clears them faster.",
      oathbroker: "Meat Toss: each co-signatory but the shield receives a separate account, payable apart, with a lien of grease left where it was settled. We settled out by the walls. The lien is not perpetual: it shrinks and dims, and fire discharges it at once.",
      ironpalm: "A steak hung over each of the others. Each walked alone to the edge, and the grease stayed there, where nobody needed to stand. The stain faded in the end, shrinking and dimming. Fire would have finished it sooner.",
      ranger: "Steak over my head. Walked to the wall, clear of the others, dropped it there. Puddle stays out of the lanes. Remember where. It shrinks and dims in time; fire clears it at once.",
      shadowblade: "A steak dangled over my head, terribly forward. I carried it off to a quiet corner and left my little grease puddle where nobody walks. Tidy. It shrinks and dims in time, a shame; fire would take it faster still.",
      pyromancer: "A steak hung over my head like a prize! I carried it to the wall and dropped my grease there, because grease and I are a bad combination. It shrinks and dims after a while, or burns off if fire reaches it. I would have liked to help it.",
      thermist: "Steak over me. Cold read: walk to the edge, apart. Drop it. Grease stays, and fire will burn it, so out of the way is the only way. The stain fades in time, dimming and shrinking first. Everything cools eventually.",
      driftblade: "A steak hung over me, so I carried it round through the gaps between the others to the far wall and dropped it there, out of every path I'd want to run later. The stain shrinks and dims in time, and fire clears it faster.",
      marionettist: "A steak lowered over each player but the lead. Each exits to the wings, apart from the others, and drops the grease offstage. The stains shrink and dim with time, or burn away if fire reaches them. (The Doll, carefully not underneath anything.)",
      cleric: "The steaks hang over everyone but our knight. Each of us went to our own place by the wall, so no two puddles touched, and the grease stayed where nobody had to walk. It dims and shrinks in time; fire takes it sooner.",
      grovekeeper: "Meat Toss sows grease, dear. Each of my lot carried theirs to the edge of the field and planted it there, apart, where it wouldn't spoil the paths. It lingers, shrinking and dimming; fire clears it at once.",
      chronomancer: "Steaks over all but the knight; each walks to their own spot by the wall and drops it. The grease stays, shrinking and dimming a little with every beat, and fire clears it at once. I chose my spot last time; I chose it again.",
      alembist: "Steaks over all but our shield, each leaving grease and then a stain that waits for fire. Each to their own spot by the wall. Grease is a reagent; I labelled where it went. It shrinks and dims in time, and fire ends it at once.",
      threadweaver: "Steaks over my stitches. I loosened every thread so each could walk to the wall alone and leave their grease out of the way. The stain shrinks and dims in time, or burns away if fire reaches it. Then back together.",
    } },
    'Family Meal': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A silver cloche came down over one of ours. One plate for the whole table. The line gathered under it and shared the blow, and I joined them the moment I could leave my post. Split, it was light work.",
      gloomsworn: "A silver cloche came down over one of our own, and one plate does not feed a table. We went under it together and split the blow, and I stood in the ring too. Split, it is a small thing for each.",
      thresher: "A silver cloche came down over one of our number, and one portion for the whole table is lighter when split. We all walked in under it, as mourners to a heavy bier, and the weight went light.",
      bloodreaver: "Family Meal! One big plate, split between us all. We crowded in under the cloche, shoulder to shoulder, and I made sure to be there for my share. It hurts less shared. Pity.",
      runeguard: "A silver cloche lowered over one of us is a clause that names the whole company. All gather beneath it and divide the blow; the fewer present, the heavier each share. I checked my arithmetic twice.",
      gorgemaw: "A platter for the whole table, under a silver lid! My friends gathered beneath the cloche and I joined them, and we shared it out. I would have eaten it all alone, frankly. Rich, but shared.",
      oathbroker: "A joint liability, under a silver cloche. All the undersigned gathered beneath it and divided the sum between us; any who absent themselves raise the share of the rest. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The cloche fell over one of us. It asked for all of us. We gathered and answered together, and the weight was small.",
      ranger: "Cloche over one of us. Everyone into it. Shared the hit. Hate crowding. Necessary.",
      shadowblade: "A cloche descended over a friend, and the whole company had to crowd in beneath it to share the portion. Not my usual company, but I squeezed in. One must dine with someone.",
      pyromancer: "Everybody crowded under the silver cloche to share the blow, which is a very cosy way to be hit. This time I singed somebody else's sleeve, for a change.",
      thermist: "Cloche over one of us. Gather in, close, closer. The blow landed split between all of us and I barely warmed. Together is a lovely temperature.",
      driftblade: "The cloche came down over one of us and we all had to stand still under it together, which I despise, so I circled until the last breath, slipped in through the gap and took my share.",
      marionettist: "An ensemble number: a silver cloche over one member of the company, and everyone onstage beneath it, sharing the line. No soloists tonight. (The Doll, front and centre, naturally.)",
      cleric: "Family Meal is served to one, and the rest of us must come to the table, or that one takes it all. I gathered everyone in under the cloche and we shared it. Grace before meals, as my mother said.",
      grovekeeper: "Family Meal is a harvest for the whole table, dear. My lot gathered close under the cloche and split it between us, roots together. Nobody went hungry, nobody fell.",
      chronomancer: "The cloche goes down over one; on the beat everyone is under it with them, and the blow is split. I was there first. I usually am. It is the only advantage of the job.",
      alembist: "One heavy portion, served to one. Dilution works on most things, so everyone went under the cloche and it divided between us. Note: it works on soup as well. I checked on the way out.",
      threadweaver: "A cloche came down over one of my stitches, and I drew every thread tight to bring the whole hem in under it. Shared among us all, it barely stung.",
    } },
    'Larder Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He hurled casks at the two of ours standing farthest off. They stepped out from under before it landed and left the casks on open floor. Solid cover. We kept them for the next blast, and I counted them like rations.",
      gloomsworn: "Casks came flying at the two of us standing furthest out. We sidestepped at the last moment and left them lying in the open, where they make shelter. Keep them away from the Cook, or he gathers them in.",
      thresher: "He flung casks at the two of us farthest out. Whoever's it was led it onto open ground, stepped off at the last, and left it standing: good shelter later. Not near Grubnak, mind, or he catches it.",
      bloodreaver: "Barrels, thrown at whoever stood furthest off! They dodged; pity, a barrel to the face is honest work. The casks stayed where they fell, and those were worth keeping.",
      runeguard: "The shelf is torn loose and a circle trails the two farthest of us. Carry it somewhere useful, step aside before it lands, and keep the cask; it is shelter for what follows. Written in the margin: not near him.",
      gorgemaw: "He flung his own larder at the two guests farthest from the table. They let the casks land beside them, not on them, and kept them as furniture. Poor service. Good barrels.",
      oathbroker: "Larder Toss: delivery to the two co-signatories farthest from the Chef. They signed for it at a sensible address, stood aside on arrival, and kept the goods. The fine print: the cask is collateral against the roar.",
      ironpalm: "He threw a cask to the farthest of us. They stepped aside. It stayed. Something to stand behind, later.",
      ranger: "Cask came at me for standing furthest out. Led it to open floor, stepped off at the last. It stays. Good cover. Not too near the ogre, or he catches it.",
      shadowblade: "A cask thrown at me for keeping my distance, which is hardly fair. I placed it neatly on open ground, stepped aside, and kept it. A lady needs something to hide behind.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cask at me! Me! I led it somewhere sensible, out of the fire, hopped clear, and then had a lovely barrel to hide behind for the roar. Splinter-free.",
      thermist: "I'm farthest, so a cask is mine. Lead it to clear floor, out of the fire, not too near him. Step off. It sat there, solid, and I knew exactly what was coming. Steadier already.",
      driftblade: "A cask came for me because I had drifted farthest out, and I was glad of the company: I swung it round to open floor, slipped from under at the last breath, and left it standing, which mattered a moment later.",
      marionettist: "Props flung from the larder at the two players farthest upstage. They step aside on cue and leave the scenery standing; it matters in the next scene. My Doll admired the set dressing.",
      cleric: "The two who stand farthest from him have a cask thrown at them. They led it to open floor, stepped out before it fell, and left it standing, and I blessed those barrels later, when they saved us. Not near the cook, mind; he gathers them in.",
      grovekeeper: "Larder Toss plants casks in the field. Whoever's farthest out leads them to good ground, steps aside before they fall, and lets them stand. Cover is planted like a hedge, dear.",
      chronomancer: "Casks at the two farthest, a beat after the shelf comes loose. Step aside before it lands and leave it where it's useful. The roar comes after. It always comes after.",
      alembist: "Casks thrown at the two of us farthest off. Step out from under, but land them somewhere useful. Note for the shelf: casks are not waste. They are shelter. Keep them, and do not sit on the full ones.",
      threadweaver: "The two stitches farthest out got a cask each. I let their threads out so they could lead the casks to clear floor and step aside, and then we had something to hide behind. Slack, used on purpose, is no sin.",
    } },
    'Gut Blast': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He roared across the whole kitchen. No shield blocks that. I put a cask between myself and Grubnak like the rest of the line and kept still behind it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He roared through the whole kitchen, and only something solid turns that. A cask went between me and him, and the others crouched behind their own. Even I hide from some things.",
      thresher: "His belly swelled and I knew that look, a bull before a bellow. It reaches the whole kitchen, and only something solid stops it, so I sat behind a cask, or the pot later, with him on the far side. Quite cosy.",
      bloodreaver: "He bellowed so hard it shook the walls. Even I hid behind a barrel for that one; it is a scar with nothing in it. Cask between us, then back to work.",
      runeguard: "His belly swells and the breath is drawn deep; the blast leaves a shadow behind anything solid. A cask between oneself and him, or the pot later, and the sentence cannot reach. I told the others where to kneel.",
      gorgemaw: "Revolting manners: the ogre belched at the entire dining room. I ducked behind a cask with my friends, him on the far side, and let the stench pass. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "Everyone not sheltered owes the roar. The clause exempts any party behind a cask, or later the pot, with the Chef on the far side. We found shelter, and I filed a note on the quality of the casks.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. I stepped behind a cask. He shouted. It went past me, as wind goes past a stone.",
      ranger: "Roar covers the whole kitchen. Only cover is a cask, or the pot later. Put it between me and the ogre. No line for once, only a wall.",
      shadowblade: "When the ogre drew breath to roar, I tucked myself behind a cask with it squarely between us. A roar cannot touch what it cannot find.",
      pyromancer: "The loudest thing I have ever heard, and I once blew up a library! Hide behind a cask, or the pot later, with Grubnak on the far side. Hair blown back. Not burnt.",
      thermist: "Find a cask. Put it between me and him. Low. Then the roar came and its heat went round me on both sides and never touched me. Too close, but not too far.",
      driftblade: "His roar fills the whole room, so the only answer is a cask, and I swung in behind one with him on the far side and stayed perfectly still while it howled round me.",
      marionettist: "His great aria: a roar that fills the whole house. The company takes cover behind the scenery, the casks, with the star on the far side. My Doll hid behind a barrel and mouthed along.",
      cleric: "When he draws in that great breath, everyone must hide behind a cask with him on the far side. Anyone caught in the open is hurt terribly. I steered our people to the barrels and counted heads twice, as one does in the small hours.",
      grovekeeper: "Gut Blast blows like a gale off the moor, dear. My lot sheltered in the lee of the casks, or the pot when there was one, with him upwind. Every windbreak counts.",
      chronomancer: "Belly swells, a long held beat, then the roar. Cask between you and him before the beat ends. I learned that the hard way once. Once was enough, and I made sure of it.",
      alembist: "One roar, whole kitchen, and the only remedy is a cask, or the pot after the fire, between you and him. Note: each blast cracks the casks, and on the harder nights a cask shelters only two.",
      threadweaver: "He drew breath and I pulled every one of my stitches behind a cask, him on the far side. Anyone left hanging out in the open would have heard from me afterwards.",
    } },
    "Butcher's Charge": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lowered his head at me and charged till he hit something. I put bare brick behind me, stepped aside at the last, and he hit the wall and stood dazed. The line kept off his path. Not the stove, not the casks.",
      gloomsworn: "He lowered his head at me and ran. I had bare brick at my back, waited, and slid aside at the last breath. He met the wall and stood dazed, and the others kept off his road. I have been a door for worse.",
      thresher: "He put his head down at our knight and ran like a bull through a fence. I kept off that line, and when our knight led him into the brick and he stood there dazed, that was my moment for the cut. His hour, entirely.",
      bloodreaver: "He lowered his head and came for me, my friend! I led him at bare brick and stepped aside, and he hit it like a drunk. Stuck and dazed, so I carved into him. Not the casks, not the stove.",
      runeguard: "His head lowers toward me and a path is drawn to the far wall. Aim it at brick, never the stove or our casks, and step aside once it sets. Dazed, he bleeds more freely. I rather enjoyed the geometry.",
      gorgemaw: "Headfirst at me, like a bull at a gate. I stood with bare brick at my back and stepped aside, and he ate the wall instead. Stuck, dazed, and still not full. A fitting course for him.",
      oathbroker: "The Chef runs on until met by something solid, and the terms do not say what. I nominated bare brick, neither the stove nor our casks, and stepped aside once the terms were fixed. Dazed, he defaulted.",
      ironpalm: "He lowered his head at me. I gave him a wall to speak to, then stepped aside. He listened to the wall for a while.",
      ranger: "It lowers its head at the knight and runs until it hits something. Keep off the line. Brick stops it dazed. Good time to shoot. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Our knight led the brute head-down into a brick wall. Delightful. The rest of us kept off the line, and while he sat there dazed I took my time with his back.",
      pyromancer: "He charged our knight, who aimed him at bare brick! Not the stove, not the casks. Everyone kept off his line, and then he was stuck there seeing stars, and I poured everything into him.",
      thermist: "His line runs to the knight. Off it. Brick behind the knight. Then he went, hot and fast, and hit the wall, and I was all over him while he swayed. Overheated, delighted.",
      driftblade: "Head down and straight as a thrown spear, and bare brick at the end of it, so I cut through off his line and watched him meet the wall. Then I circled him while he saw stars, a hawk over a sleepy field.",
      marionettist: "The charge: the star runs at the lead player, who steps aside and lets him meet the back wall of the set. Not the stove, not the scenery. (The Doll, applauding the stunt work.)",
      cleric: "He lowers his head at our knight and runs until he hits something. Our knight learned to lead him into bare brick, where he stands dazed, and everyone else kept clear of his path. I said a small prayer for the brick.",
      grovekeeper: "The charge is a bull through a fence, dear. Our shield turned him toward bare brick, not the stove or the casks, and my lot stepped off his path. He stood dazed after, like a struck ox.",
      chronomancer: "Head down, the path follows the knight, and a beat before he goes it stops following. Aim at brick, step aside on that beat. He was dazed, as I had known he would be. A lovely long pause.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one ogre in a straight line until he hits something. Remedy: aim him at bare brick, off our line, never the stove or our casks. The stove erupts; I wrote that one twice.",
      threadweaver: "He charges our knight until he hits something. I pulled my stitches off his line, and our knight brought him round to a bare wall, not the casks, not the stove. Straight lines are easy to avoid.",
    } },
    'Chop Chop': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver glinted, and the ladle or lid on his back said what came after. We crossed to the dull half, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two orders, one drill.",
      gloomsworn: "A glint on one cleaver, and a ladle or lid on his back to follow. We moved to the dull side first, then out for the ladle or in for the lid. I read both before taking a step.",
      thresher: "A glint on one cleaver and a tool on his back behind it: two appointments, and I like to see both before I attend either. Dull half first, then out for the ladle or in for the lid.",
      bloodreaver: "Chop, then whatever's on his back! Glinting half first, so I took the other side. Then the lid meant in, the ladle meant out. Two lessons in one breath, my friend.",
      runeguard: "He speaks twice: the glinting cleaver names the half, and the tool on his back names what follows. The dull half first, then in for the lid or out for the ladle. A two-line verse, and I had it by heart.",
      gorgemaw: "A two-course dish: first the minced half, then whatever hung on his back, ladle or lid. I sat on the dull side, then took the right seat for the second. Hurried, but edible.",
      oathbroker: "A compound clause: the glinting half is due first, and the tool on his back states the terms of the second, ladle without, lid within. I read both before signing either. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke twice. The glint: this half. The tool: near, or far. I answered both.",
      ranger: "Glinting side gets chopped. Then the tool on its back: ladle, out; lid, in. Read both before moving.",
      shadowblade: "Two announcements at once, how showy: the glinting cleaver for the half, the tool on his back for what follows. I took the dull half, then followed the tool.",
      pyromancer: "Two dishes at once! The glinting cleaver chops a half, then the tool on his back follows: ladle, get out; lid, get in. I managed both and only dropped one spell. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Glint, half; back, tool. Dull side first. Then hot: the ladle meant out, so out, and then straight back in, burning. The rhythm of it made me giddy.",
      driftblade: "The glint tells you which half and the tool on his back tells you what next, so I went through to the dull half and out of the ladle's ring and back in through the steam.",
      marionettist: "A scene with a quick change. The glinting cleaver strikes one half of the stage, then the tool on his back sets the next cue, ladle out, lid in. (The Doll, hitting both marks.)",
      cleric: "Chop Chop asks two things together. The glinting side is chopped, then the tool on his back follows: the ladle wants us out, the lid wants us in. I watched each friend get it right, and was prouder than I should be.",
      grovekeeper: "Chop Chop is a frost and then a thaw, dear. The glinting half falls first, then the tool on his back tells the weather: ladle, keep out; lid, come in. My lot read both before moving.",
      chronomancer: "Chop, then the tool a beat behind. The glint picks the half, his back picks in or out. A two-bar phrase. I have heard it so often now that I hum it.",
      alembist: "Two labels at once. First: the glinting cleaver, that half chopped. Second: the tool on his back, ladle out, lid in. Read both labels before pouring. I once skipped one, and the ceiling still has the mark.",
      threadweaver: "The glinting half first, then the tool on his back: out for the ladle, in for the lid. I moved my stitches as one piece, twice, and dropped none. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Order Up!': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One plate each, laid round the kitchen and served in turn. I took mine and stood on it like a sentry on a painted square until it was dished. None left unmanned, and none left on the counter.",
      gloomsworn: "Plates were laid round the kitchen, one for each of us, served in turn. Each kept a plate and stood on it until it was taken. I wait well. The dead have practice.",
      thresher: "Plates went down all round the kitchen, one apiece, the fullest rim served first. I stood on my own and waited to be served, which is more patience than most guests show.",
      bloodreaver: "A plate for each of us, served in turn round the room. I took mine and stood on it until the rim ran full. An empty plate left alone hurts everyone, and that's not a scar worth having.",
      runeguard: "Cloches round the kitchen, their rims filling at different rates; the fullest is served next, working round from the top left. Each claims one plate. I claimed mine early and marked it with a rune.",
      gorgemaw: "Plates for every guest, laid round the room. I took mine, of course, as did my friends, each of us standing on our own until served. A plate left on the counter is an insult to the whole kitchen.",
      oathbroker: "One plate per co-signatory, served in sequence round the room, the fullest rim next. The fine print: an unclaimed plate is charged to all of us. So we each signed for one, and stood on it.",
      ironpalm: "Plates around the room, one for each of us. Each stood on their own until it was served. None went unanswered.",
      ranger: "One plate each, round the room from the top left, fullest rim first. Stood on mine. Counted heads against plates twice.",
      shadowblade: "Plates laid out round the kitchen, one per guest. I chose mine, stood on it until it was served, and nobody's plate went uncollected. One does have standards.",
      pyromancer: "Plates! A ring of them, one apiece, served round from the top left, fullest first. I stood on mine and cast. An empty plate hurts everyone, which is rude of it.",
      thermist: "Plates, one each. Know yours before it starts. Fullest rim lands first. Then rush: ran to mine, stood still till it went, ran off. No plate left cold.",
      driftblade: "A plate each, all round the kitchen, and I had to stand on mine and wait to be served, which is the worst thing a person can ask of me. Then up and through and away to the next gap.",
      marionettist: "Every player has a plate, a mark, and a cue: laid round the stage and served in sequence, the fullest rim next. Stand on your mark until served. My Doll stood on hers, beaming.",
      cleric: "One plate set before each of us, and the whole room served in turn. I walked among them, making sure each friend stood on theirs, because a bare plate would have hurt us all.",
      grovekeeper: "The order rail is a row of seedlings coming up one at a time, dear. Each of my lot tended their own and stood on it till it bloomed. Fullest rim first.",
      chronomancer: "Plates in a ring, each rim filling toward its own beat, served round from the top left. Each of us on our own plate when it's served. I knew which was mine, having stood on it before.",
      alembist: "One plate per guest, served in order round from the top left, fullest rim first. Assign the plates beforehand. An unserved plate spoils for everyone, and I have the burn scars to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "A plate for each stitch, served round the room in turn. I let the threads out so everyone could reach their own and stand on it. Nobody's plate left bare.",
    } },
    'Rolling Meatballs': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lobbed meatballs against the walls. Each wobbled with its lane drawn across the room, then rolled straight. I kept my footing out of every lane until the last had passed. Ground between lanes is good ground.",
      gloomsworn: "Meatballs wobbled at the walls and rolled straight, each lane drawn across the floor. I stood between the lanes and let them pass. It is the one fair thing in that kitchen: it tells you where death will roll.",
      thresher: "Meatballs rocked against the walls, each with its path drawn straight out ahead of it. I stood between two paths and watched them go by. A tidy kitchen, for such a messy cook.",
      bloodreaver: "Giant meatballs, rolling across the room! I'd love to be hit by one, but it knocks you sideways and that's undignified. Out of their lanes until they pass.",
      runeguard: "Each meatball lands at the wall and wobbles, its lane drawn across the kitchen before it rolls. Stand clear of every lane until all have passed. It is nearly polite of him to underline his own sentences.",
      gorgemaw: "Meatballs rolled across the dining room instead of being served. I would have eaten them had they held still. Stood between the lanes and watched them go by. Overcooked, and impolite.",
      oathbroker: "Each meatball is a debt in transit along a fixed route, drawn before it moves. The undersigned stood outside every route and paid nothing, which is the finest sum.",
      ironpalm: "Round things rolled toward us. Each showed its path first. I stood where none would go, and let them speak to the wall.",
      ranger: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll straight across. Lanes drawn. Stood between them, a few paces off the nearest. Easiest shooting of the night.",
      shadowblade: "Meatballs, bowled at us one by one. Each sits at the wall and wobbles before it rolls, showing its path. I stood between the paths and watched them go by. Very rude, and very predictable.",
      pyromancer: "Enormous meatballs rolling straight across the kitchen, and each one wobbles first to show its lane. I stood clear and kept casting. Not flattened. Hair: gravy, again.",
      thermist: "Meatballs at the walls, wobbling, lanes drawn. Stand between. Then they rolled, hot and fast, and I did not move a hair until the last one passed. Pulse high, hands cold.",
      driftblade: "The meatballs wobble at the walls and then roll straight through the room, so I wove between the lanes and through behind each one as it passed, and never got flattened once.",
      marionettist: "Enormous meatballs rolled across the stage, each along a lane marked in advance. The company stands between the lanes and does not improvise. The Doll was narrowly missed. Gasps from the house.",
      cleric: "The great meatballs wobble against the walls before they roll across the room. One knocked our archer clean off the plate, and I mended the poor thing and scolded gently. Stand out of the lanes, dears.",
      grovekeeper: "Rolling Meatballs come down their lanes like a spring flood down the furrows, dear. Read where each one wobbles and stand between the channels. My lot stayed dry.",
      chronomancer: "They land, they wobble, a beat, then they roll, one after another. Stand between the lanes until the last has passed. I had counted them before the first landed.",
      alembist: "Meatballs, large, rolling. Stand outside every lane; each wobbles first and shows its path. Note: when they come with the plates, the plates sit between the lanes. Kept the leftovers for study.",
      threadweaver: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll straight across. I let my stitches out between their lanes and gathered them back once the lanes had gone quiet. Easy needlework, with a bit of nerve.",
    } },
    'Flare-Up': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Cook heaved his pot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen caught for good. We were off that ground before it lit, mended against the heat. A smaller parade ground after, but I only need enough floor to stand on.",
      gloomsworn: "He heaved the stockpot on the stove and the top of the kitchen caught, for good. We were off that end before it lit, and braced for the heat. A smaller room, but all of us in it.",
      thresher: "He heaved the stockpot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen went up in flame, and it stays burning. I had walked down out of that end before it caught. A smaller kitchen after, but room enough to work.",
      bloodreaver: "He set his own stove alight, the whole top of the room! The heat reached everyone, a good warm scar. Then I stayed out of the fire for the rest of the night. Burn once, learn.",
      runeguard: "The stockpot is lifted toward the stove. Abandon the top of the kitchen before it catches, for it burns from then on, and have everyone whole for the heat that follows. I drew a line across the floor plan.",
      gorgemaw: "The cook scorched his own kitchen and left us standing in the smoke. Out of the top of the room before it caught, then. The heat seasoned everyone. Badly burnt, and all of it his fault.",
      oathbroker: "The top of the kitchen is foreclosed for the remainder of the service, and the heat is owed by all. The undersigned vacated before it caught, and counted it prudent.",
      ironpalm: "He set the stove alight. The top of the room burned. We had left before it spoke. The heat reached us anyway, as a shout reaches the next room.",
      ranger: "Pot onto the stove. Top of the room burns, for good. Out of it before it catches; paced the new edge afterwards. Smaller room. Fewer lanes.",
      shadowblade: "Halfway through, the mark heaved his pot onto the stove and set the top of the kitchen ablaze. I had already left that end of the room. Fire is so terribly common.",
      pyromancer: "He set his own kitchen on fire! Very good fire, too, the colour of real trouble. Out of the top of the room before it catches; it never goes out. I was jealous. Cloak singed.",
      thermist: "Fire all along the top of the room, roaring, and I ran so hot I nearly forgot to move. Cold now: leave the top before it catches. It never goes out. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "He heaved the pot onto the stove and the whole top of the room went up, so I ran down and away before it caught, and now the room is small and I resent every wall of it.",
      marionettist: "The interval: the ogre sets the upper stage alight for the rest of the run. The company moves downstage before it catches, and the heat reaches the stalls anyway. (The Doll, smoking slightly.)",
      cleric: "When he lifts the pot onto the stove, everyone must leave the top of the kitchen; it burns from then on. The heat reaches all of us anyway, so I had everyone mended first. A little warmth is no sin.",
      grovekeeper: "Flare-Up is the summer wildfire, dear. The top of the kitchen burns from then on, and its heat reaches us all. I moved my lot down the slope before it caught and planted before the heat.",
      chronomancer: "Pot on the stove, a beat, then fire across the top for the rest of the fight, and a wave of heat. We had left the top by then. We were all very punctual.",
      alembist: "The top of the kitchen is ablaze for good, and the heat reaches everyone. Out before it catches, everyone mended first. Note: smaller workshop now, and I miss the shelves at that end.",
      threadweaver: "The whole top of the kitchen alight, and it never went out. I drew my stitches down out of it before it caught, and we took the heat close together. Hems burn first, so keep them tucked.",
    } },
    'Flambé': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Brandy laid in stripes and lit one after another. I stood where the fire would come last, then took the ground behind the flame. Old grease I kept my boots off; boots are the one kit I cannot requisition.",
      gloomsworn: "Brandy ran across the floor in stripes and the fire took them one by one. I stood where it would come last, and when it had passed I walked onto the ashes. The old grease I would not touch; it remembers.",
      thresher: "He laid brandy in stripes and lit them one after another. I waited in a stripe that would go late, then walked onto the one already burnt out; nothing left to catch there, and no hurry. The old grease I kept clear of.",
      bloodreaver: "Stripes of brandy, catching one after another! I waited, then stepped in behind the flames onto ground already burnt. Grease stains in a stripe go up too. Kept my boots off them.",
      runeguard: "Brandy in stripes, a blue flame running along each before it catches. Step into the stripe that has just burnt, behind the fire, and keep clear of old grease. A fire reads from one end to the other like anything else.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole floor flambéed, stripe by stripe. Pretentious. I stepped into each stripe once it had burnt and kept off the old grease. Too much brandy, too little steak.",
      oathbroker: "The stripes come due in sequence, and a stripe already paid owes nothing further. The undersigned moved onto settled ground, clear of the grease liens, which remember their debts.",
      ironpalm: "Brandy on the floor. The stripes caught, one by one. I stood where the fire had already spoken, and it had nothing more to say.",
      ranger: "Stripes of brandy. They catch in turn. Step into the one already burnt. Keep off grease.",
      shadowblade: "Brandy splashed in stripes and lit in turn. I waited for one to burn, then stepped into it, behind the fire. The grease stains catch too; I avoided them, naturally.",
      pyromancer: "Flambé! Stripes of brandy going up one after another! Absolutely beautiful. Step into the stripe that's just burnt and keep off any old grease in it. I wanted to stay and admire. Singed, worth it.",
      thermist: "Brandy, stripes, flame all down the room, and I was burning just watching. Cold: wait. Step into the one that just burnt. Off the grease stains. Steady now.",
      driftblade: "The stripes catch one after another, so I ran through behind the fire, into each burnt stripe as it went and round the grease stains, never landing anywhere that hadn't burnt yet.",
      marionettist: "A pyrotechnic interlude: brandy across the stage in stripes, lit in sequence. The company steps into the ones already burnt and keeps off the grease. (The Doll, flame-resistant. Mostly.)",
      cleric: "He splashes brandy across the floor in stripes, and they catch one after another. Wait, then step onto ground that has already burnt; any grease in a stripe goes up with it. A kinder fire than most.",
      grovekeeper: "Flambé burns like stubble fires down a field, row by row, dear. Wait, then walk onto the burnt row behind the flames. The old grease we sowed earlier burns too, so keep off it.",
      chronomancer: "The stripes catch in order, a beat apart. I step into each the moment after it's burnt, a tune I was humming before it started. Old grease catches when its stripe does.",
      alembist: "Brandy in stripes, catching in turn, and every grease stain in them lights. Step into a stripe that has already burnt. Note: this is exactly how flambé is meant to work, and I am furious he does it better.",
      threadweaver: "Stripes of brandy caught one by one, so I pulled my stitches into the burnt stripes behind the flames, well away from the old grease. The cleanest pass of the night.",
    } },
    'Stockpot': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle. It is solid. It stops a charge and shelters from his roar. Marked it as ground to work from. A fixed point is worth any number of good intentions.",
      gloomsworn: "When the fire was done he dragged the pot to the middle of the room. It does not move. It stops his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I fought near it. A thing that will not move is a comfort.",
      thresher: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle and left it, solid as a tomb. It turns his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I learned to work near it. Mind its boiling, though; that comes in its own hour.",
      bloodreaver: "A pot hauled into the middle of the room, solid as a wall. Good shelter when he roars, and something to ram his face into later.",
      runeguard: "He leaps to the centre with the stockpot, and there it stays: shelter from his roar, a place to aim his charge, and a source of rings to read. It is the first fixed word on a page of moving ones.",
      gorgemaw: "At last a centrepiece: a stockpot dragged to the middle of the table, solid enough to shelter behind and something to drive him into. The broth smelled of nothing. Bland.",
      oathbroker: "A new fixture in the premises: the stockpot, placed in the middle for the remainder of the service. Solid, a shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. The fine print: it boils over.",
      ironpalm: "He set the pot in the middle. It does not move. Something to stand behind. Something that boils.",
      ranger: "Pot in the middle. Solid. Cover from the roar, a post to aim the charge at. Watch its rings. Marked it on my map and drew a circle round it.",
      shadowblade: "A pot, dragged to the centre and abandoned there. Solid, wide, opaque. A lovely thing to stand behind. Its rings boil next, though.",
      pyromancer: "He dragged a giant copper pot into the middle of the floor, and it's solid! Something to hide behind when he roars. And it bubbles. I like it. I would like to marry it.",
      thermist: "Pot in the middle. Solid. Cover. Then the copper went hot and the rings began to boil off it. Watch them. I stood too close and my glass read nonsense.",
      driftblade: "The pot goes to the middle and stays there, which I can respect in a pot. Round it, behind it, and later through its rings, like a swift through a bell tower.",
      marionettist: "A new set piece, centre stage: the stockpot. Solid scenery, cover for the roar, a target for the charge. Rings boil off it later; do not lean. (The Doll, leaning on it, very casual.)",
      cleric: "After the fire he hauled the great pot to the middle of the kitchen and left it there. It shelters us from his roar, and our knight can lead his charge into it. The rings it gives off I like less.",
      grovekeeper: "The stockpot settled in the middle like a boulder after a landslide. Solid ground to shelter behind and a place to steer his charge. Rings boil off it after.",
      chronomancer: "Pot to the middle, a leap and a thud. It stays. Cover from the roar, a wall for the charge, and the rings come next, out then in. A fixed note in a restless piece.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: the stockpot, now in the middle. Uses: shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. Hazard: it boils over in rings. Label both, and do not lick the rim.",
      threadweaver: "He planted that pot in the middle for good. Something to tie the hem behind when he roars, and our knight can lead his charge into it. Finally, a fixed point to sew to.",
    } },
    'Simmer': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The pot boiled over in rings, out from the rim and then back in. I stood clear of each until it had boiled, then stepped in behind it. Step, wait, step. Easy ground, if you count.",
      gloomsworn: "The pot boiled in rings, going out and then returning. I waited just beyond a ring and moved in as it passed over. Arrive after the blow, as the dead do.",
      thresher: "Rings boiled out of the pot and back in again, like a pond with a stone dropped in it. I waited outside each and stepped in as it passed. Quiet ground behind a wave.",
      bloodreaver: "Rings of boiling water off the pot, out and then back in. I waited, then followed each ring just after it boiled. Barely touched me. Shame.",
      runeguard: "The coils go dull, then red, then white, ring by ring: the colour is the clock. Step into the ring that has just boiled, outward on the first wave, inward on the second. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The pot boiled over, ring after ring, out and then in. I followed each ring once it had boiled. The broth was thin and the service slow. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "The rings come due in order, outward and then inward. A ring already paid owes nothing, so the undersigned stepped into each behind it. Receipts for each wave, I regret, were not issued.",
      ironpalm: "Rings left the pot, then returned. I waited at the edge of each and stepped in as it passed. Water is patient, until it is not.",
      ranger: "Rings out from the pot, rings back in. Stepped in behind each as it passed. Watch the colours.",
      shadowblade: "The pot boiled over in rings that went out and came back. I waited for each wave to pass and slipped in behind it, as I do. Patience is a shadow's best friend.",
      pyromancer: "The rings round the pot go dull, then red, then white, and I loved every colour. I followed each one after it boiled, out and then back in. Like a dance. A hot dance. Toes: unsinged.",
      thermist: "Rings: dull, red, white. Wait for white. Step into it. The next one boils behind me and I'm already on to the next, out and back in. Every ring a degree wiser.",
      driftblade: "Rings boiled outward from the pot, and I went out behind each, through it as it finished, and when they came back in I went back through them the other way.",
      marionettist: "Ripples of boiling water from the pot, out then in, as choreographed as any waltz. The company steps into each ring just after it boils. (The Doll, dancing.)",
      cleric: "The pot's rings boil one after another, out and then back in. I led our people into each ring just after it boiled, and nobody was scalded. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Simmer comes like ripples in a pond after a stone, dear. Step into each ring just after it passes, out first, then in. My lot rode the ripples.",
      chronomancer: "Rings out, a beat apart, then back in. I step in behind each as it passes; the second wave is the first one backwards. I noticed that before it happened, which is cheating, but quietly.",
      alembist: "Ailment: rings of boiling water from the pot, outward, then inward. Remedy: step into each ring just after it boils. Note: a pot simmering is how most of my mistakes begin.",
      threadweaver: "The rings boil out from the pot and back in. I kept my stitches walking just behind each ring as it boiled, no one ahead of the thread. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Whisk': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cross of beams through him that turned, a notch at a time. I kept to the gap between beams and walked round with it. Slow work. A turning wall is only a very orderly parade.",
      gloomsworn: "Beams crossed in him, turning slowly one notch at a time. I found the open wedge and went round with it. Slow things suit me.",
      thresher: "He had a cross of beams through him, turning a notch at a time, slow as a clock hand. I found a gap between two arms and strolled round with it. No hurry; it never hurries.",
      bloodreaver: "A cross of light swung round the room, a notch at a time. I walked with the gap and took no wound. Embarrassing.",
      runeguard: "Wire loops form a cross through him and turn by stages. Stand in the gap between its arms and walk with the turn, keeping pace. Pleasingly regular; I could have set my watch by it.",
      gorgemaw: "Air, whisked in a cross through the room, turning as he went. I walked round with the gap. All air, no substance. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "A cross of beams that turns by stages, and a gap in it for the careful. The undersigned kept to the gap and moved with each turn. The fine print: walk with it, never against it.",
      ironpalm: "The cross turned. I turned with it. It did not touch me.",
      ranger: "Cross through it, turning. Stay in the gap. Walk with the turn, never against. Like tracking a hawk that circles the same pole.",
      shadowblade: "A cross of beams, turning slowly through the room. I stayed in the gap and walked round with it, the way one keeps to the edge of a gaze.",
      pyromancer: "The Whisk! A cross of beams through him, turning step by step. I walked with the gap and kept casting, dragging my feet only slightly. Hem: intact.",
      thermist: "Cross through him, turning. Stand in the gap. Walk with it. Then the heat, cutting him all the way round, rushing the whole circle. Dizzy. Delighted.",
      driftblade: "A cross of beams turning through him, and I turned with the gap, round and round, a falcon on a thermal, never once landing, which is exactly how I like it.",
      marionettist: "The revolving set: a cross of beams turns through the ogre, a notch at a time. The company walks with the gap and takes its bows on the move. (The Doll, keeping step.)",
      cleric: "His whisk turns a cross of beams through the room, step by step. Walk round with the gap between them; our archer walked the wrong way once, and I thanked the light the poor thing lived.",
      grovekeeper: "The Whisk turns like a weathervane in a steady wind, dear. Stand in the gap and walk with it. My lot kept pace.",
      chronomancer: "A cross through him, turning a notch each beat. Walk with the gap, on the beat. The spin was the same last time. It will be again.",
      alembist: "A cross of beams, turning in steps. Stand in the gap and go round with it, the way it turns. Write it on the label. Stir with, never against.",
      threadweaver: "A cross of beams turning through him, a notch at a time. I walked my stitches round with the gap like thread winding onto a bobbin, all together, nobody lagging. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Spice Rack': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Jars of spice poured on set spots, and one followed each of the others. They carried theirs clear of everyone and stepped off before it poured. Then ladle or lid. I held the middle and kept my eyes on his back.",
      gloomsworn: "Spice rained on fixed spots, and a jar chased each of us about the floor. We drew ours away and slid aside before it poured. Ladle or lid came after. Two dangers, one watch.",
      thresher: "Jars of spice hung over certain spots, and one hung over me and followed like a crow. I led mine away from everyone and stepped aside before it poured, then looked to his back for ladle or lid. Two appointments, one evening.",
      bloodreaver: "Spice jars poured all over the floor, and one over each of the soft ones. They moved off theirs before it poured. Then ladle or lid, straight after. Spicy!",
      runeguard: "Jars shake over set places, and one over each of us not before him. Carry yours to clear floor and step off before it pours, then heed the tool that follows. The fixed spots I copied into the plan; the wandering jars I trusted to the others.",
      gorgemaw: "He seasoned the floor, and each guest besides. My friends led their jars away and stepped aside before they poured, then ladle or lid. Over-seasoned, and not a grain on the meat.",
      oathbroker: "A fixed pattern of pours, plus one account per co-signatory but the shield. Each carried theirs clear and stepped off before payment fell due, and then looked to his back for the ladle or the lid.",
      ironpalm: "Jars followed the others across the floor. Each led theirs to empty ground and stepped aside before it poured. The jar fell on nothing. Then the ladle spoke, or the lid.",
      ranger: "Jars over set spots, one over me. Moved mine clear of the others, stepped off. Then ladle or lid. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Spice jars shaken over the floor and one over me personally. I took mine somewhere empty and stepped aside before it poured. Then the ladle or the lid, as ever.",
      pyromancer: "A jar over my own head, and more over the floor, as if I were a roast. I walked mine clear of the others and dodged before it poured. Then in or out. Sneezing, but unsinged.",
      thermist: "Jar over me. Carry it clear. Off it before it pours. Then hot again, in or out for the ladle or the lid, and nose running.",
      driftblade: "A jar hung over me, so I carried it through the room away from everyone and slipped off before it poured, then out or in for the ladle or the lid.",
      marionettist: "Jars of spice over set marks, and one over each of the company. Each carries theirs offstage and exits before it pours; ladle or lid follows. (The Doll, sneezing.)",
      cleric: "The spice jars pour on the same places each time, and one follows each of us but our knight. Carry yours away from everyone, step off before it pours, then mind the ladle or lid.",
      grovekeeper: "Spice Rack sows on set rows, dear, and one seed follows each of my lot. Carry it to empty ground and step off before it drops. Ladle or lid comes straight after.",
      chronomancer: "Jars on set spots, one following each of us; it stops following a beat before it pours. Step off then. Ladle or lid right after. I have danced this one.",
      alembist: "A jar over each of us but our shield, plus fixed spots on the floor. Carry yours to clear floor, step off before it pours. Note: a wasteful use of good spice, and I say that as someone who has set fire to cinnamon.",
      threadweaver: "A jar followed each of my stitches; I let them out apart so no two poured together, then gathered them in for the ladle or lid. Two jobs, one tidy seam.",
    } },
    'Boil Over': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He boiled over, and the closer you stood, the worse it scalded. We all got as far from him as the room allowed, me included. A post is no use if you are cooked at it.",
      gloomsworn: "He boiled over; the closer, the worse the scald. We all went to the far side of the kitchen, me included. A post can be left briefly, when the post itself burns.",
      thresher: "He boiled over, and the nearer I stood the worse it scalded. So I walked to the far wall, as far as the kitchen goes, and came back to him once it settled.",
      bloodreaver: "Boil Over! Closer means hotter. I wanted to stand next to him for it. I didn't. Ran to the far wall like everyone else, and still came away with a good scald.",
      runeguard: "Rolling heat and bubbles gather about him, and the harm falls away with distance. All of us to the walls, then, as far from him as the room allows. A short rule, and nobody argued with it.",
      gorgemaw: "As every bad cook eventually does, he boiled over. Closer was hotter, so even I backed off to the walls. A scalding broth, and no one to thank for it.",
      oathbroker: "The debt scales with proximity to the Chef. The undersigned all retreated to the walls to reduce the sum, myself included, and I do not mind admitting that I ran.",
      ironpalm: "The heat rose from him. Near, it shouts. Far, it whispers. I went far.",
      ranger: "Closer to it, worse it burns. Ran to the far wall. Kept shooting. Wind carries heat, but not that far.",
      shadowblade: "He boiled over, and the closer one stood, the worse it scalded. So I put the whole kitchen between us. Distance is its own kind of shadow.",
      pyromancer: "Boil Over! The closer you stand, the more it scalds. I'm always at the back anyway, so I went even further back. Ankles slightly poached.",
      thermist: "Heat, rising, too much; I ran hot and ran far. Cold read after: the closer, the worse. The far wall is the only answer. My glass agreed.",
      driftblade: "The nearer you were the worse it burned, so I ran, through and away to the far wall, faster than I have ever run, and a hawk would have called it graceful.",
      marionettist: "A great swell of steam from the star; the nearer the footlights, the worse it scalds. The company retreats to the back wall and watches the rest from there, the Doll steamed pink.",
      cleric: "When the heat rolls off him, the nearer you stand, the worse it is. Everyone, even our knight, ran to the walls, and I mended them before it came. A retreat is no shame.",
      grovekeeper: "Boil Over is a heatwave from the hearth outward, dear. Get as far from it as you can. My lot ran to the walls, and I planted on them before it broke.",
      chronomancer: "Heat swells off him, a long beat, then it boils over. Every step away is a step less burnt. We were all at the walls by the end of the beat.",
      alembist: "Scalding heat, worse the nearer you stand. Distance, all of us, to the walls, and a dose ready before. Note: boiling over is the worst thing a pot can do. I know. I have been the pot.",
      threadweaver: "The nearer to him, the worse the scald, so I let every thread out to the walls, our knight's too, and pulled them back once it had boiled. Even a good seam needs room.",
    } },
    'Last Orders': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the trolley came silence, then Last Orders on the whole room. No step escapes it. We gathered by the healer, mended in the quiet, and braced shoulder to shoulder.",
      gloomsworn: "The trolley, a long hush, then Last Orders on all of us. Nowhere to step. We pressed round the healer in the hush and were mended ahead of it. I make good use of silence.",
      thresher: "A hush after the trolley, the kind you get before a roof gives way. We gathered round our healer in it and got mended, so Last Orders found us ready.",
      bloodreaver: "Last Orders: a scorcher across everyone. We came in close, got mended in the quiet before it, and took it together. A good one! The best one.",
      runeguard: "A hush follows the trolley; then he calls it. Use the silence to gather and mend before the blast that reaches all of us. Silence, in his kitchen, is a punctuation mark, and I have learned to read it.",
      gorgemaw: "After the trolley the cook paused, then called Last Orders and threw the last of his kitchen at everyone. We gathered, mended, and swallowed it together. A filling course.",
      oathbroker: "A final bill, payable by all, presented after a period of grace. The undersigned used that grace to restore their means, then settled together, to the last coin.",
      ironpalm: "Silence. Then he called to everyone at once. We had gathered in the silence. We answered.",
      ranger: "Quiet after the trolley. Then the big one hits everyone. Heal in the quiet. Take it together. Hate waiting. Worth it.",
      shadowblade: "After the trolley, a lull, and then the mark called Last Orders and hit everyone at once. I spent the lull being mended. Even shadows need a little care.",
      pyromancer: "The quiet after the trolley was eerie, and then Last Orders hit the whole room at once! We were together, and mended first. I nearly lost my eyebrows entirely.",
      thermist: "Cold in the quiet: gather, mend, wait. Then the blast came hot and loud across all of us, and we held. Steady, for once, from first degree to last.",
      driftblade: "A hush after the trolley, and I had to stand still with everyone, ugh, and then the whole room was hit and we went through it together, and I could finally breathe.",
      marionettist: "A pregnant pause after the trolley, then his final call to the whole house. The company gathers and is restored during the pause, and holds its breath on cue. (The Doll, bracing dramatically.)",
      cleric: "After the trolley there was a hush, and I used every moment of it. When Last Orders came, everyone was close and whole, and we came through it. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Last Orders is the first frost, dear: you know it's coming, and you have the quiet before it. I gathered my lot and planted on them all in that quiet.",
      chronomancer: "Silence after the trolley; the blast comes after it, every time. We gathered in the silence and were whole by the bell. I was ready. I am nearly always ready after the fact.",
      alembist: "One great blast at everyone, after a long silence. Use the silence: everyone mended and gathered. Note: the shelf should still hold something by then. Mine held two vials and a biscuit.",
      threadweaver: "In the quiet after the trolley I drew the whole hem in close and mended every stitch, right to the selvedge. Then Last Orders hit us all, and the seam held.",
    } },
    'Gorge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "At the end he just ate, and every mouthful hit all of us, harder each time. Nothing to hold but the line. We had to put him down fast. Held, long enough.",
      gloomsworn: "In the end he only ate, and each mouthful struck every one of us harder than the last. Nothing to hide behind, so we killed him fast. The dark kept me upright to the end.",
      thresher: "At the end Grubnak only ate, and every bite came in on all of us, heavier each time. Nothing to dodge, so I put everything into the cut and brought him in before he finished his supper. A hungry man, courteously ended.",
      bloodreaver: "Gorging, every bite hitting us all, harder each time! Wonderful. But you cannot outlast it, so we hit him with everything until he fell.",
      runeguard: "He eats. Each bite reaches us all and grows heavier; it cannot be outlasted, only ended. Strike with everything that remains. The last page is always shorter than one hopes.",
      gorgemaw: "He began to eat his own cooking, and every bite hurt his guests more. A rival at the table! I made very sure he did not finish his meal.",
      oathbroker: "A compounding debt, each instalment heavier than the last, owed by all. It cannot be serviced for long; only foreclosing on the Chef settles it. We foreclosed.",
      ironpalm: "He ate. Each bite louder than the last. We could not wait for him to stop. So we ended the conversation.",
      ranger: "Every mouthful hits everyone, each one worse. Shoot faster. Held my breath between draws, which helped nobody.",
      shadowblade: "At the end the mark simply ate, and every bite hurt everyone more than the last. No shadow helps with that. One just finishes the job quickly.",
      pyromancer: "At the end he just ate and ate, and every bite hit all of us harder! No dodging, only burning, so I burned brighter than I ever have. Scorched, but triumphant.",
      thermist: "Each bite hotter than the last. Cold read: it does not stop. Hot answer: everything I have, now. My glass cracked on the last bite, which seems fair.",
      driftblade: "He just ate, and every bite hit us harder, and there was nowhere to run through to. So I spent every ounce of speed I had on him.",
      marionettist: "The finale: the ogre devours the scenery, and every bite hurts the whole company more. The only way out is to bring the curtain down quickly. (The Doll, striking with feeling.)",
      cleric: "At the end he ate, and every bite hurt all of us more. There is no enduring it for long; I kept them standing while the others struck him down. He was only hungry. We all end that way.",
      grovekeeper: "Gorge is the long winter, dear: each bite colder. No crop outlasts it, so I kept my lot rooted while they cut him down quickly.",
      chronomancer: "Each bite on the beat, harder each time. No undoing that for ever. So the others hurried, and I kept time.",
      alembist: "Every bite he takes hurts all of us more, and there is no remedy, not for long. Empty the shelves and end him. I threw everything I owned, including the vial I was saving for my birthday.",
      threadweaver: "He ate, and every bite tugged at every stitch, harder each time. A thread won't hold for ever, so we cut him down quickly, before anything frayed.",
    } },
    'Clogged Drains': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Grease pooled round the drains for good, and any fire lights it. I marked every drain on my map and kept the line off those patches. Bad footing is one thing; burning footing is another.",
      gloomsworn: "The drains backed up and the grease stayed round them. Fire will find it sooner or later, so I took the fight somewhere else and left that patch to the dark.",
      thresher: "On the harder nights the drains backed up and grease pooled round them and stayed, a dark standing water. Any spark sets it burning. I kept off that ground all evening and worked the drier floor.",
      bloodreaver: "The drains backed up and spat grease round them for good. It burns when fire finds it. I kept my boots off it. A fine scar is one thing; burning feet are another.",
      runeguard: "A gurgle from the drains, and the grease pools round each and stays; when fire comes, it burns. Mark the drains, keep off their grease. I added each to the floor plan in red.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder nights the drains back up, pooling grease round them for good. A filthy kitchen. Harmless until fire reaches it, then I keep well clear. Disgusting.",
      oathbroker: "A permanent lien of grease round each drain, which comes due when fire reaches it. The undersigned noted their positions, and we stay clear of the property.",
      ironpalm: "The drains backed up. The grease stayed. When the fire came, it spoke through the grease. We stood elsewhere.",
      ranger: "Drains back up. Grease round each, for good. Fire lights it. Keep off it. Mapped them.",
      shadowblade: "On the harder nights the drains overflow with grease, and it stays. Any fire sets it alight. I keep well away from the drains, like a cat avoiding puddles.",
      pyromancer: "Grease round the drains for good, and grease plus fire equals me on fire, so I keep away from the drains. Hair: safe, for now. His stove, on the other hand, is a disgrace.",
      thermist: "Grease round the drains, for good. Harmless while cold. The moment fire reaches it, it burns. Off it before then. A stable system waiting for its spark.",
      driftblade: "The drains spill grease that stays, so I run round them now rather than through, which goes against my nature and my boots.",
      marionettist: "The drains overflow with grease, a permanent stain on the stage. Fire lights it, so the company keeps its marks elsewhere. (The Doll, wrinkling its painted nose.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the drains overflow and grease pools round them for good. When any fire reaches it, it burns. I kept everyone off the grease by the drains.",
      grovekeeper: "The drains flood with grease like a field after a storm, dear, and it stays. When the fire comes, it burns. My lot keep their roots off that ground.",
      chronomancer: "The drains back up and the grease stays. It's harmless until the fire comes. Then it's not. I remember where the drains are the way one remembers a bad day.",
      alembist: "Grease round the drains, permanently. Harmless alone; with fire, it burns. Keep off it when fire is coming. Note: a reagent waiting for its partner. I have felt that.",
      threadweaver: "The drains overflowed and grease ringed them for good. Fire lights it. I kept my stitches away from the drains so nothing burned. Stains and fire both ruin good cloth.",
    } },
    'Belly Flop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He flopped down where he stood, which is a thing he does on the harder nights. Out from under him, and open floor at my back, because the shock threw us. Then ladle out, lid in. A wall behind you is worse than the blow.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights he dropped flat on the spot. I moved out from beneath him with clear floor at my back, because the shock throws us, then read his back for ladle or lid. I know what falling feels like.",
      thresher: "He simply lay down on the spot like a felled tree, on the hard nights. I scrambled out from under with open floor behind me, since the shock shoves, and read his back: ladle, out; lid, in.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one flopped belly first, my friend! Out from under, then thrown back; open floor behind me, not a wall. Then the tool on his back said in or out. A fine fall.",
      runeguard: "His shadow swells beneath him, and the tool on his back names the sequel. Out from under, open floor behind to land on, then in or out as the tool decrees. I now check my back for walls before I check my front.",
      gorgemaw: "Belly first onto his own floor. Out from under him, with open floor behind me for when the shock threw me. Then ladle or lid. Overweight, both of us.",
      oathbroker: "Liability for those beneath him, and a throw for everyone. The fine print: be thrown onto open floor, not into a wall or a cask. Then the tool on his back sets the next terms.",
      ironpalm: "He fell. I moved out from under. The shock pushed me back, and I let it, toward open floor. Then the ladle spoke.",
      ranger: "Flops where it stands. Out from under. Thrown back, so open floor behind. Then the tool on its back: ladle out, lid in.",
      shadowblade: "He flopped down on his belly, terribly undignified. Out from under, thrown backward, so nothing hard behind me. Then the tool on his back tells me where to be.",
      pyromancer: "He belly-flopped, all of him at once! I ran out from under and let the shock fling me onto open floor, because a wall at my back would have been worse. Then ladle, out; lid, in. Pride: bruised.",
      thermist: "Out from under. Open floor behind me. Then the shock, a scalding fall, and the tool on his back says in or out. All the weight of him, delivered at once.",
      driftblade: "He flops where he stands. I went out through his shadow before it hit, and the shock threw me across open floor, a stone skipped on a pond, then in or out for the ladle or the lid.",
      marionettist: "A pratfall, which the house adored: out from under, the company thrown backward onto open stage, not into the scenery. Then the tool on his back cues the next scene. (The Doll, thrown, unbroken.)",
      cleric: "He flopped flat on the spot and the shock threw everyone back. Out from under first, with open floor behind, since walls and casks hurt, and then the ladle or the lid on his back. A hard fall, gently taken.",
      grovekeeper: "Belly Flop is an earthquake, dear. Out from under, and keep open ground behind for the shock to throw you onto. Then the tool on his back says in or out.",
      chronomancer: "He flops, a beat, then the shock throws us. Out from under first, open floor behind. Then the tool on his back on the next beat. I will have heard this one before.",
      alembist: "One ogre, flopping. Out from under, open floor behind for the throw. Then the tool on his back: ladle out, lid in. Note: casks behind you hurt. I know the exact bruise.",
      threadweaver: "He flopped and the shock threw my stitches apart, so I made sure each had open floor behind. Then in or out for the tool on his back. I will not have them landing in a wall like dropped buttons.",
    } },
    'Cleaver Toss': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He sent cleavers down lines at the two farthest from him. They turned the lines clear of us, stepped aside, and the steel came home the way it went. Stay off both ways. Thorough.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of us, farthest out, had cleavers sent along a line. Each turned theirs from the rest and stepped aside, and the blades came home by the way they went. I stayed off the line, out and back.",
      thresher: "He threw cleavers down lines at the two of us farthest off, and they come back up the same line. Each walked their line clear of the rest of us and stepped off it, and I kept off both ways. A line is a line, coming or going.",
      bloodreaver: "Cleavers thrown at the two furthest off, along lines! Off the line. Then they come back along the same line. I've had a cleaver in the back before; overrated.",
      runeguard: "Score marks run from him to the two farthest of us. Point the line away from the others, step off as it sharpens, and stay off: it returns the same way. The second reading is the one I nearly forgot.",
      gorgemaw: "The cook threw his cleavers at the two farthest guests and then called them back. We pointed the lines away from each other, stepped off, and waited. A tired dish.",
      oathbroker: "A debt delivered along a fixed line and returned along the same. The fine print is the return. The undersigned stepped off and stayed off, and the Chef collected nothing in either direction.",
      ironpalm: "He threw. It came along a line. I stepped off. It went back. I stayed off.",
      ranger: "Lines to the two farthest. Aim it clear of the others. Step off. It comes back the same way. Stay off.",
      shadowblade: "Cleavers thrown at the two of us farthest out. I aimed mine away from friends, stepped off, and waited; it came back the same way. Persistence is so tiresome.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cleaver at me for being far away, which I always am! Point it away from the others, step off, and stay off; it flies back the same way. Sleeve, nicked.",
      thermist: "I'm farthest. Line from him to me. Aim it clear, step off. Hot: it flew past, then back, and I didn't move until it was home.",
      driftblade: "A cleaver line followed me because I'd drifted farthest out, so I pointed it away and slipped off it and stayed off while it went out and came back through.",
      marionettist: "Cleavers thrown along lines at the two players farthest upstage, then back along the same. Each points their line offstage, exits it, and stays out. (The Doll, ducking twice.)",
      cleric: "He throws cleavers along lines at the two farthest of us, and they fly back the same way. Point them away from everyone, step off, and stay off until they're home.",
      grovekeeper: "Cleaver Toss is a boomerang, dear. The two farthest point their lines away from my lot, step off, and keep off until it comes home.",
      chronomancer: "Cleaver out along its line, then back along it. The second pass is the one people forget. I remembered, having forgotten it already.",
      alembist: "Two cleavers, two lines, out and back. Point yours away from the others, step off, wait for it to come home. Note: they return. Things always return, especially the ones I throw away.",
      threadweaver: "Two cleaver lines to my farthest stitches. I had them point their lines away from the hem, step off, and stay off till the cleavers flew home. Out and back, like a needle.",
    } },
    'Mise en Place': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver toward one side of its board. I put the order and the side in my book. Nothing hit us. It always comes later; that is what laying out means.",
      gloomsworn: "Three tools were set on the pass, left to right, the cleaver nearer one end of its board. I recited the order and the side to myself. All of it returns. I keep things.",
      thresher: "Three tools laid out in a line along the pass, left to right, the cleaver nearer one end of its board. Nothing hurt yet. I tucked the order and the side away to keep.",
      bloodreaver: "Tools laid out like he was planning something: cleaver, ladle, lid, in some order, and the cleaver lying to one side. Remember it. He serves them later.",
      runeguard: "Three tools laid upon the pass, left to right, the cleaver resting toward one side of its board. Record the order and the side; they return later in that sequence. This is the table of contents.",
      gorgemaw: "Tools set out on the pass, left to right, as if preparing a banquet, the cleaver lying to one side. I committed the order to memory. Pretentious, but it is the one menu I trust.",
      oathbroker: "The Chef lays out the terms of a later service, left to right, the cleaver lying to one side. The undersigned read every clause, and then, for safety, read them again. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "He laid out three tools, left to right. I listened. He would speak them later.",
      ranger: "Three tools on the pass, read left to right. Cleaver lies to one side; marked which. They come back.",
      shadowblade: "Tools laid out very carefully on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I watched, as I watch everything, and remembered.",
      pyromancer: "He arranged his tools on the pass like a display! Left to right, cleaver to one side. Memorise it; it comes back much later. I wrote it on my sleeve. Which then caught fire.",
      thermist: "Cleaver, ladle, lid, left to right, in some order. The cleaver's side. Remember. It all comes back much later. Cool head, cool ledger.",
      driftblade: "Three tools on the pass and nothing else happening, which bored me, but I read them anyway, left to right, and which side the cleaver lay, and kept the lot in my head while I ran.",
      marionettist: "Props set out on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side: the script for a later scene. The company learns its lines. My Doll memorised hers in the wings.",
      cleric: "Three tools laid out on the pass, and the cleaver set to one side of its board. I tried to hold the lot in my head, and later he served them in that order. An old man's memory is a candle; I shielded it with both hands.",
      grovekeeper: "Mise en Place is the sowing, dear; the harvest comes later. Read the tools left to right and the side of the cleaver, and keep it in mind.",
      chronomancer: "Three tools, left to right, and the cleaver's side. I will need that later; I needed it last time.",
      alembist: "Specimens on the pass: three tools, laid left to right, the cleaver to one side. Serving order, I suspect. Labelled on my wrist, since the shelf was elsewhere.",
      threadweaver: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I stitched the order into my memory, in that order, and bit the thread.",
    } },
    'Service!': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The tools came off the pass in the order he had laid them. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle means out, the lid means in, one after another. A drill I had been handed in advance.",
      gloomsworn: "The order on the pass was the order we lived. Cleaver for its half of the floor, ladle for out, lid for in. I called each a breath ahead so nobody had to guess.",
      thresher: "Cleaver, ladle, lid: he served them in the order he had laid them out, and I took them one by one. Cleaver for its half, ladle to get out, lid to get in. Never late.",
      bloodreaver: "Service! In the order he had laid them: cleaver's side chopped, ladle sent us out, lid drew us in. Good rhythm, my friend. I enjoyed it.",
      runeguard: "The sign was given long before: the order on the pass. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle sends us out, the lid draws us in, each in turn. As foreseen; and I did foresee it.",
      gorgemaw: "He served his laid-out tools in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, the ladle outward, the lid inward. A tasting menu. Sent most of it back.",
      oathbroker: "The terms laid out earlier come due in order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle without, lid within. The undersigned met each in turn, and I initialled every margin.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the tools in their order. Cleaver, ladle, lid. We answered each.",
      ranger: "Tools in order from the pass. Cleaver's side chopped. Ladle, out. Lid, in. Moved in sequence.",
      shadowblade: "Service! and the tools came out in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. I moved through them like a dance I had rehearsed, and he never once looked at the floor.",
      pyromancer: "The tools came out in the order he had laid them, and I barely had time to cast between: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. Singed, but on time.",
      thermist: "The order, remembered. Hot: cleaver, cross; ladle, out; lid, in, one after another, burning through it. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The tools came off the pass in the order he laid them, and I flew the course: through the cleaver's half, out for the ladle, in for the lid, one after another.",
      marionettist: "The scene written earlier on the pass now plays. The cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. The company hits every cue. (The Doll, flawless.)",
      cleric: "Service! The tools came out one by one in the order he had laid them: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. We moved together and I counted aloud for the slow of heart.",
      grovekeeper: "Service! is the harvest of what he laid out, dear. The tools come in the order we read: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. My lot reaped it in turn.",
      chronomancer: "Service! The tools come in the order they were laid, a beat apart: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. I had heard the melody long before he wrote it.",
      alembist: "Three tools in the order laid out. The cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in, in turn. Note: the label from earlier paid off. I may frame it.",
      threadweaver: "The tools came out in order, and I moved my stitches with each: the cleaver's side cleared, out for the ladle, in for the lid. Another neat seam.",
    } },
    'Dinner Rush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Hung plates over the four of us, set where we stood, the nearest served first. My post was the first: his east flank, near enough to smell the stove. The second sat west, inside the lid's quiet; the last two stayed out past the ladle. I never left his side.",
      gloomsworn: "A plate hung over each of us, set down where we stood, the nearest dished up first. I took his east side, then the west edge of the lid's quiet, beside the second plate. The far two stood out past the ladle. I do not mind standing near what wants me dead.",
      thresher: "Hot plates came down over every head wherever we happened to stand, and he serves the nearest first, each in turn. I took the east flank while his cleaver chopped the west, then walked round inside the lid's quiet. The far pair waited past the ladle.",
      bloodreaver: "A plate wherever we stood, the nearest served first! I took the first at his east side as the cleaver chopped the west, then went to his west inside the lid's quiet, beside the next plate. Then out for the ladle. A lovely rush!",
      runeguard: "Four plates, four places, and the order is a matter of distance: the nearest is served first. One goes at his east flank, one at his west where the lid's quiet thins, and two out beyond the ladle. A plate set too near comes up too soon. I drew the whole as a small map.",
      gorgemaw: "A dinner rush: a hot plate over every guest, nearest served first. I ate the first at his east elbow, then sat in at his west, inside the lid's quiet, beside the second plate rather than on it. Two more far out past the ladle.",
      oathbroker: "A plate is issued to each of the undersigned where they stand; the nearest falls due first. The fine print is the order: first at the Chef's east flank, second on his west at the lid's quiet edge, the last two beyond the ladle's reach. Settle in that sequence.",
      ironpalm: "He hung a plate over each of us. Where we stood, it stayed. The nearest spoke first. East of him, one of us. West of him, inside the lid's quiet, another. Two far out, beyond the ladle. We did not hurry the order.",
      ranger: "Plates land where we stand, nearest served first. Close pair took his east flank, then his west, just inside the lid's ring. Mine set far out past the ladle's reach. Served last.",
      shadowblade: "Hot plates over each of us, nearest served first, an order I admire. I set mine at his east flank, close enough to hear him breathe, and the next went in the lid's quiet on his west. The far pair stayed out past the ladle.",
      pyromancer: "Hot plates over our heads, set down wherever we stood, served nearest first! Mine went far out past the ladle with the other caster, so I stood there and burned until the very last beat while the close pair took his east and west sides. Not a flame wasted.",
      thermist: "Plates where we stand, nearest served first. Cold arithmetic: one east of him, one west at the lid's quiet edge, two far out beyond the ladle. Then hot, quick, and I was at the far end, watching the close pair work. A degree from disaster.",
      driftblade: "Plates came down where we stood, nearest first, and I went round his east flank as the cleaver took the west and slid through to his west, tucked in the lid's quiet, then out past the ladle in one long run.",
      marionettist: "Blocking, last scene: hot plates on each player's mark, nearest served first. East side of the star for the first, his west at the lid's quiet edge for the second, and two far upstage past the ladle. My Doll and I took upstage, served last, and were reviewed kindly.",
      cleric: "He hung a hot plate over each of us wherever we stood, and served the nearest first. East, toward the sunrise, for the first; the second just inside the lid's quiet on the sunset side; mine far out, past the ladle. Our archer set hers too near once, and it came up out of order.",
      grovekeeper: "Dinner Rush is sowing in rows, dear: each plate falls where you stand, and the nearest ripens first. East furrow first, west furrow second where the lid's quiet thins, the last two out past the ladle. Row by row, and nobody trampled.",
      chronomancer: "Three beats, the nearest plate first. His east side on the first, as the cleaver takes the west; his west side on the second, just inside the lid's quiet; the far two together on the third, past the ladle. Set one too near and it comes up off the beat.",
      alembist: "Dosage by distance: a hot plate for each of us, nearest served first. First dose at his east side, second at his west on the lid's quiet edge, last two far out past the ladle. Note: the first patient is too full for another, so leave room beside the second.",
      threadweaver: "Hot plates pinned over every stitch where it stood, the nearest served first. East side of him for the first, west side inside the lid's quiet for the second, the last two out beyond the ladle. The near pair went east, then in, then out. The far pair never budged.",
    } },
  },
};
